### `VE-M1A-WINDOWS-001` — inert control-plane and canonical Windows policy

- Commands: `.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest tests\test_control_plane.py -q`; `.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m unittest tests.test_control_plane -v`.
- Result: pytest exit 0, **31 passed, 1 skipped, 52 subtests passed**; unittest exit 0, **32 tests passed, 1 skipped**. The sole skip is the POSIX-only mode assertion on Windows.
- Verified contract: M1a remains explicit-opt-in and absent from startup; its empty schema, exact manifest, behavioral constraints, integrity checks, rollback, locking, owner-only managed ACLs and multiprocess initialization pass. Windows private-root resolution requires native `SHGetKnownFolderPath(FOLDERID_LocalAppData)` and has no environment fallback. Ancestor write trust is limited to current/SYSTEM/Administrators/canonical profile owner plus exact enabled, non-deny-only `S-1-15-3-*` SIDs reported by `TokenCapabilities`.
- Live canonical result: **`BLOCKED_BY_PLATFORM`** in this managed sandbox. Native LocalAppData resolved to `C:\Users\ppetr\AppData\Local`; the current token exposed no enabled capability SID matching the canonical ancestor's capability write ACE. Validation raised typed `ControlPlanePathError` before creating `C:\Users\ppetr\AppData\Local\Cyryx Labs\Onyx` or `control_plane.sqlite3`.
- Interpretation: this is successful fail-closed policy enforcement, not proof that canonical activation works under the intended installed-user token. The inert schema remains verified through isolated test roots and is not operationally activated.

